  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe key insight is that the overlap integral resembles autocorrelation, suggesting that h should be designed to minimize its peak cross-correlation with shifted versions. Building on the previous approach, we propose a multi-faceted strategy combining global optimization with structured perturbations.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \nWe formulate the problem as minimizing max_k [\u222bh(x)(1 - h(x+k))dx] with constraints:  \n- h(x) \u2208 [0, 1]  \n- \u222bh(x)dx = 1  \n\nUsing a discretization of n_points, we represent h as a vector with sum exactly n_points/2. The overlap integral is computed via convolution.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \nThe plan consists of:  \na. Initialization: Use `initial_h_values` with added small structured perturbations.  \nb. Global optimization: Alternate between Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) and L-BFGS-B, leveraging PSO's global exploration and L-BFGS-B's local refinement.  \nc. Perturbation: Apply periodic structured perturbations favoring low-correlation regions.  \nd. Fitness evaluation: Compute C\u2085 using the given evaluate function, ensuring feasibility.  \n\n4. Search choices and variations  \n- Population size for PSO: 20-30 particles.  \n- Inertia weight: Adaptive,Starting at 0.7, decreasing to 0.4.  \n- L-BFGS-B iterations: 100 per PSO iteration.  \n- Perturbation strength: Varies by inverse square root of time.  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \nExpected runtime per particle iteration: ~60s. With 100 particles and 100 iterations, total budget is under 600s.  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \nEnsure feasibility via normalization checks and validate candidate h against constraints and target budget.",
